SCRIPTS=[
["China’s EV boom became a race for survival.","A 2018 report put the number of electric-vehicle manufacturers at nearly five hundred."],
["The cost was enormous.","Byton reportedly raised eight point four billion yuan without reaching mass production.","Saleen received six point six billion in state-backed investment, yet reportedly sold just thirty-one Maimai cars."],
["Around 2014, policy support and Tesla’s patent pledge helped fuel optimism.","Entrants from technology, property and consumer electronics saw an opportunity.","But entering the market was easier than mastering automotive manufacturing."],
["Venture capital backed ambitious growth stories.","Local governments offered land, facilities and industrial funding, seeking jobs, tax revenue and economic development.","But financing a factory was only the beginning."],
["For some startups, fundraising moved faster than manufacturing readiness.","Launch events promised the future, while production schedules slipped.","Suppliers, quality control and working capital proved harder to manage than a compelling presentation."],
["From 2020, shutdowns and restructurings exposed the risks.","A reported review of fifteen troubled EV companies put disclosed financing above one hundred billion yuan, alongside substantial idle production capacity."],
["By 2025, brands including Leapmotor, Xpeng, Li Auto, Xiaomi and NIO remained prominent competitors.","Their delivery scale and financial performance differed.","Staying in the race did not mean achieving sustainable profitability."],
["Price competition put margins under pressure.","For buyers and investors, the question was no longer simply who could deliver cars, but who could sustain the business behind them."],
["Three lessons: verify manufacturing capability, examine funding obligations, and assess margins alongside sales scale.","For overseas buyers, supplier continuity matters."],
["Follow UNILAND MOTORS for Chinese vehicle sourcing and market insights."]]
